> Timer logic all seems fine. I have to admit I still don't understand the point of maintaining a driver exclusively for use by qemu (vs. just using Systick or whatever, which is a driver for real hardware that qemu happens to emulate).

@andyross , in Cortex-M0 ARCHitecture the SysTick peripheral is optional and nRF51 (which is the SoC QEMU emulates) does not have it. So this was never an option. QEMU _should_ be supporting the nRF RTC peripheral; then we could just use the nRF RTC timer. But QEMU guys only chose to support the TIMER peripheral; hence, I had to write this driver myself. :( 

I think @stephanosio has implied that we could help QEMU to support RTC for nRF51 - then we could get rid of this driver. Could be future work!

> I have quite a few ARM-related upstream QEMU patches that I have to revisit some time in the future. I might have a go at it when I do that later; after all, the nRF RTC peripheral looks simple enough.

@stephanosio , the HAL API of the RTC is pretty much similar to TIMER (that's why it was rather easy for me to write the driver).
So I guess supporting RTC in QEMU should be straightforward. If you have any patch about this, share with me, and I could assist with reviewing and testing it. Thanks in advance.

